<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Townshend Acts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1767">1767</a>–<a href="/years/1768">1768</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Townshend duties</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Townshend Acts were a series of laws passed by the British Parliament in 1767 and 1768, named after the chancellor of the exchequer Charles Townshend, that imposed duties on glass, lead, paint, paper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> imported into the American colonies. Colonial boycotts led to the repeal of all the duties except that on tea in 1770, which later provoked the Boston Tea Party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/townshend-acts.jpg" alt="Townshend Acts" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joshua Reynolds · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CharlesTownshend.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> the British government was deep in debt, and Parliament sought to raise revenue in the colonies, first with the Sugar Act 1764 and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamp-act-1765">Stamp Act 1765</a>, which was repealed the following year together with the Declaratory Act's claim that Parliament could legislate for the colonies in all cases whatsoever. The Chatham ministry concluded that colonists who had objected to a direct, internal tax would accept indirect, external duties on imports, and Townshend's program rested on that belief. Historian John Phillip Reid called it a mistaken belief of vital importance in the events leading to the Revolution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Five acts are usually counted. The Revenue Act and the Commissioners of Customs Act passed on 29 June 1767; the first laid the duties and allowed colonial supreme courts to issue writs of assistance to search private property, the second created an American Board of Customs Commissioners of five members based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>. The New York Restraining Act and the Indemnity Act followed on 2 July 1767, the one barring New York's assembly from passing bills until it complied with the Quartering Act 1765, the other cutting duties on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> tea. The Vice Admiralty Court Act of 8 March 1768 added courts at Boston, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> and Charleston that judged smuggling cases without a jury.</p><p>The revenue was meant to pay the salaries of colonial governors and judges, so that they would remain loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, and the acts were also to establish the precedent that Parliament had the right to tax the colonies. Townshend himself died suddenly on 4 September 1767, before the last act was introduced.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>John Dickinson's twelve Letters from a Farmer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, appearing from December 1767, argued that any tax laid by Parliament for revenue was unconstitutional. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> merchants agreed to stop importing certain British goods from 1 January 1768, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> and George Mason organised a boycott in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>; British exports to the colonies fell by 38 percent in 1769, but the movement faded by 1771. In Boston the seizure of John Hancock's sloop Liberty on 10 June 1768 set off riots, and troops were sent to the city. Lord North's partial repeal received royal assent on 12 April 1770, but historian Robert Chaffin judged that scarcely anything had changed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Townshend Acts” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Britain's victory in the Seven Years' War, Parliament sought to tax the colonies to help pay for their defence, through the Stamp Act of 1765 and the <b>Townshend duties</b> of 1767, provoking protests against taxation without representation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
